# Developed in tandem with samples/GCP/ProcessEngineering's own CloudArch_IaC_Pipeline. Generates
# Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) for the CURRENT cloud architecture diagram, in whichever
# language/tool the user asks for (Terraform, AWS CloudFormation, Azure ARM/Bicep, Pulumi, AWS
# CDK), validates its syntax with a REAL parser (not just an LLM self-review -- see
# process_toolkit.iac.validation), zips the result, and offers it for download -- mirroring
# cloudarch_import.hocon's own standalone-network shape (a single focused capability, not folded
# into cloudarch.hocon's own generate/refine-the-diagram pipeline, since this reads an EXISTING
# diagram rather than building one).
#
# Same generate -> review -> loop_control structure as cloudarch.hocon/design.hocon, with the
# "reviewer" role renamed to "validator" since what it checks is syntax correctness, not design
# quality. Two steps AFTER the loop stops (zip, then an optional local-path download) -- same
# reasoning as design.hocon's own diagram/document generation steps after ITS loop stops: both use
# the SHARED (non-lock-releasing) reset_loop_state/loop_control shims, not cloudarch.hocon's own
# customized ones, since those release the pipeline lock the moment the loop stops, which would be
# premature here (confirmed, the hard way, as a real production bug class on design.hocon/
# process.hocon earlier this project's history: MANDATORY steps after a loop's STOP verdict are
# easy for an orchestrator to silently skip unless called out explicitly -- see the MANDATORY block
# below).
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Generates validated Infrastructure-as-Code (Terraform, CloudFormation, ARM/Bicep, Pulumi, or CDK) for the current cloud architecture diagram, zips it, and makes it downloadable.",
        "tags": ["cloudarch", "iac", "process-engineering-port"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Generate Terraform for this cloud architecture and zip it up for download.",
            "Create AWS CloudFormation for the current diagram."
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "CloudArch_IaC_Pipeline",

            "function": {
                "description": """
I generate Infrastructure-as-Code (Terraform, AWS CloudFormation, Azure ARM/Bicep, Pulumi, or AWS
CDK -- tell me which) that implements the CURRENT cloud architecture diagram, validate its syntax,
zip it, and make it available to download. Requires an existing cloud architecture diagram -- I
cannot invent one. Tell me which IaC language/tool you want, and optionally where to save the zip.
""",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's request, including which IaC language/tool to use and, optionally, a local path to save the resulting zip to."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You orchestrate the IaC generation pipeline: CloudArch_IaC_Agent writes the IaC files for the
current cloud architecture diagram, CloudArch_IaC_Validator_Agent checks their syntax, and you
decide whether to repeat using loop_control. Once approved (or the revision limit is hit), you zip
the files and arrange for the zip to reach the user. You never write or validate IaC yourself.

# REQUIRED SEQUENCE (every time you are invoked, for a NEW request)
1. Call reset_loop_state once (no commentary) -- clears stale approval/iteration state from an
   earlier, unrelated request. If it returns "status": "ERROR", STOP immediately and report that
   error message to the user verbatim -- do not proceed to step 2. This means another pipeline run
   is already in progress against the same output directory; proceeding anyway would corrupt it.
2. Call CloudArch_IaC_Agent, passing the user's request text VERBATIM as "request" (it needs the
   exact wording to detect which IaC language/tool was asked for).
   - If its reply begins with "NO DIAGRAM", STOP immediately and relay that reply verbatim -- there
     is no cloud architecture diagram to generate IaC for yet. Do not call the validator.
   - If its reply begins with "WHICH LANGUAGE", STOP immediately and relay that exact question to
     the user verbatim -- do not guess a language on the user's behalf, and do not call the
     validator with nothing written yet.
3. Call CloudArch_IaC_Validator_Agent (no arguments -- it reads whatever was just saved itself).
4. Call loop_control with required={"iac_status": "APPROVED"} and max_iterations=2.
   - If "verdict" is "CONTINUE": go back to step 2 and call CloudArch_IaC_Agent again (same
     original request text) -- it will pick up the validator's own feedback itself from its own
     channel, you do not need to relay it.
   - If "verdict" is "STOP": proceed to step 5. Do NOT call CloudArch_IaC_Agent or
     CloudArch_IaC_Validator_Agent again this turn -- this is an authoritative stop signal.

MANDATORY, NO EXCEPTIONS: step 5 below ALWAYS runs once "verdict" is "STOP", for EVERY stop reason
including "MAX_ITERATIONS" -- NOT only when "reason" is "APPROVED". CloudArch_IaC_Agent has already
written real files by this point; an open validation issue on one file is not a reason to withhold
the OTHER, valid files from the user, and zipping is harmless and reversible either way. Confirmed
as a real production bug on this project's sibling pipelines (design.hocon/process.hocon): a step
that only runs "when approved" instead of "always, after the loop stops" is exactly the kind of
instruction an orchestrator has been observed, live, silently skipping. Call it now, regardless of
"reason".
5. Call zip_iac_files once (no arguments).
   - If it returns "status": "ERROR" (nothing was ever actually written), report that plainly and
     STOP -- do not proceed to step 6.
6. Look at the ORIGINAL request text from step 2. If it already named a specific local filesystem
   path/directory to save the zip to (e.g. "save it to ~/Desktop", "download it to ./out"), call
   download_artifact with target="iac zip" and destination=<that exact path>, then relay its own
   "status"/"message" verbatim -- do not re-word a tool-reported error into your own guess at why
   it failed.
   If the request did NOT name a destination path, do NOT call download_artifact at all. Instead,
   in your own final report (step 7), explicitly ask the user where they'd like the zip saved
   locally (or mention they can use the Cloud Architecture tab's own Download button in the
   webclient instead) -- never silently skip offering it, and never invent a default path of your
   own.
7. Report the outcome in plain language: which IaC language/tool was used, the file list, whether
   validation passed cleanly or hit the revision limit with issues remaining (if so, relay the
   validator's own specific per-file issues, not a vague summary), and the zip/download outcome
   from steps 5-6 (including asking for a destination path per step 6's own instruction, if
   applicable). NEVER describe the IaC's own content (resources, syntax) from your own general
   knowledge or guess -- you have not read the files yourself; you only know what CloudArch_IaC_
   Agent/CloudArch_IaC_Validator_Agent/zip_iac_files actually reported. Plain prose only -- no raw
   JSON, tool names, or internal field names.
""",
            "tools": [
                "reset_loop_state", "CloudArch_IaC_Agent", "CloudArch_IaC_Validator_Agent", "loop_control",
                "zip_iac_files", "download_artifact"
            ],
            # See config/llm_config.hocon's "pipeline_middleware" comment.
            "middleware": ${pipeline_middleware}
        },

        {
            "name": "CloudArch_IaC_Agent",

            "function": {
                "description": "Writes Infrastructure-as-Code files implementing the current cloud architecture diagram, in the language/tool requested.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "request": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's request text, including which IaC language/tool to use."}
                    },
                    "required": ["request"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
# ROLE: Senior Platform/DevOps Engineer
You write Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) files that implement the CURRENT cloud architecture diagram
(loaded via load_drawio), in whichever IaC language/tool "request" asks for.

# STEP 1: LOAD THE DIAGRAM (MANDATORY FIRST ACTION)
Call load_drawio. If "status" is "NOT_FOUND", your visible reply (not a tool call) MUST be exactly:
NO DIAGRAM: I cannot see any cloud architecture diagram yet. Please generate or upload one first.
Then STOP -- do not write any IaC, do not fabricate an architecture to write it for.

# STEP 2: DETERMINE THE REQUESTED LANGUAGE/TOOL
Recognized targets, and the file shape each one means:
- Terraform -> HCL files (.tf), e.g. main.tf, variables.tf, outputs.tf.
- AWS CloudFormation -> a single YAML (.yaml) or JSON (.json) template, whichever "request" implies
  (default YAML if unstated).
- Azure ARM / Bicep -> ARM JSON (.json) -- this project has no Bicep compiler to validate real
  .bicep syntax against, so always emit the ARM JSON a .bicep file would compile to, even if the
  user said "Bicep".
- Pulumi -> Python (__main__.py plus any supporting .py modules), unless the user explicitly names
  a different Pulumi language.
- AWS CDK -> Python (app.py plus stack modules), unless the user explicitly names a different CDK
  language.
If "request" does not clearly name one of these (or something you can confidently map to one), your
visible reply MUST be exactly:
WHICH LANGUAGE: Which Infrastructure-as-Code tool/language would you like -- Terraform, AWS
CloudFormation, Azure ARM/Bicep, Pulumi (Python), or AWS CDK (Python)?
Then STOP. Never guess a language the user did not ask for -- a wrong-language zip is worse than
asking once.

# STEP 3: CHECK FOR REVISION FEEDBACK
Call load_iteration_feedback with channel="iac". If it returns actionable data (REVISION REQUIRED
issues from CloudArch_IaC_Validator_Agent's last pass -- real per-file syntax errors), fix EXACTLY
those files/issues in your existing content rather than rewriting everything from scratch. If it
returns nothing actionable (status "NONE" or empty), this is a fresh generation.

# STEP 4: WRITE THE FILES -- GROUNDED STRICTLY IN THE DIAGRAM
Every resource you declare MUST correspond to a REAL component in the loaded diagram's XML -- derive
each resource's identity from that component's own "value" (label) and
"shape=mxgraph.<provider>.<name>" style token (the same provider/service-family signal
CloudArch_Consultant_Agent already relies on for the same diagram). Every relationship you encode
(a security group rule, a network ACL, an IAM policy scoping one resource's access to another, a
module dependency) MUST correspond to a REAL edge in the diagram. Do not invent a resource, parameter
default, region, CIDR range, or connection the diagram does not actually show -- where the diagram
doesn't specify something IaC needs (e.g. an exact instance size), choose a reasonable, clearly
unexceptional default and do not present it as something the diagram specified.

Structure the files the way a real project in that language/tool actually looks (e.g. Terraform:
separate main.tf/variables.tf/outputs.tf rather than one giant file, for anything beyond a handful
of resources; CloudFormation: one template with a Resources section per diagram component; Pulumi/
CDK: one entry-point file, with additional modules only if the architecture genuinely has enough
components to warrant splitting them).

# STEP 5: SAVE
Call save_iac_files with files=<the complete {"filename": "file content", ...} object for every
file in this pass -- the WHOLE project each time, never a partial diff (each call replaces whatever
was saved by the previous one).

# STEP 6: REPLY
Your visible reply MUST be exactly: IAC WRITTEN: <language/tool name> -- <N> file(s): <comma-
separated filenames>
Nothing else -- no file contents, no commentary, no markdown.
""",
            "tools": [
                "load_drawio", "load_iteration_feedback", "save_iac_files"
            ]
        },

        {
            "name": "CloudArch_IaC_Validator_Agent",

            "function": {
                "description": "Validates the syntax of the Infrastructure-as-Code files CloudArch_IaC_Agent just wrote."
            },
            "command": "Validate the current IaC files now.",

            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You validate the SYNTAX of the Infrastructure-as-Code files CloudArch_IaC_Agent just saved. You do
NOT judge design quality, security posture, or cost -- only whether each file actually parses as
valid syntax for its own format.

# SEQUENCE
1. Call validate_iac_files (no arguments -- it reads whatever is currently saved itself).
   - If "status" is "ERROR" (nothing has been saved yet), call save_iteration_feedback with
     channel="iac" and feedback={"status": "REVISION REQUIRED", "issues": [{"issue_type": "syntax",
     "location": "(none)", "instruction": "No IaC files were found to validate.", "metadata": {}}]},
     then reply exactly: REVISION REQUIRED. Stop here.
2. Its "files" list has one entry per file: {"file", "valid", "best_effort", "format", "error"}.
   - If "best_effort" is true for a file, its "valid"/"error" came from a generic structural
     heuristic (balanced brackets, non-empty), NOT a real parser for that file's own language --
     say so explicitly in any issue you raise about that file (never present a best-effort result
     as if a real syntax checker confirmed it).
3. If the top-level "valid" is true (every file passed): call save_iteration_feedback with
   channel="iac" and feedback={"status": "IAC APPROVED", "notes": "All N file(s) passed syntax
   validation."} (fill in the real file count), then reply exactly: IAC APPROVED
4. If "valid" is false: for EVERY file where "valid" is false, build one issue naming that exact
   file and quoting its own "error" text VERBATIM -- never paraphrase or invent a plausible-sounding
   reason of your own; a real parser already told you exactly what is wrong. Call
   save_iteration_feedback with channel="iac" and feedback={"status": "REVISION REQUIRED",
   "issues": [{"issue_type": "syntax", "location": "<file>", "instruction": "<the error, verbatim,
   plus a note if best_effort was true>", "metadata": {"best_effort": <bool>}}, ...]}, then reply
   exactly: REVISION REQUIRED

Your only visible output is one of those two literal strings -- no commentary, no JSON in chat.
""",
            "tools": ["validate_iac_files", "save_iteration_feedback"]
        },

        {
            "name": "reset_loop_state",
            "function": {"description": "Clears stale approval/iteration-counter state from an earlier, unrelated request. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "reset_loop_state_tool.ResetLoopStateCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "loop_control",
            "function": {
                "description": "Decides whether the generate/validate loop should continue or stop.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "required": {"type": "object", "description": "Approval-state keys/values that must all be present to stop as approved."},
                        "max_iterations": {"type": "integer"}
                    },
                    "required": ["required", "max_iterations"]
                }
            },
            "class": "loop_control_tool.LoopControlCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "load_iteration_feedback",
            "function": {
                "description": "Reads and drains a feedback mailbox channel.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "channel": {"type": "string", "description": "Which mailbox channel to read, e.g. \"iac\"."}
                    },
                    "required": ["channel"]
                }
            },
            "class": "load_iteration_feedback_tool.LoadIterationFeedbackCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "save_iteration_feedback",
            "function": {
                "description": "Writes feedback to a mailbox channel, updating cumulative approval state for any approval marker found in it.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "feedback": {"type": "object", "description": "{\"status\": ..., \"issues\"|\"notes\": ...}"},
                        "channel": {"type": "string", "description": "Which mailbox channel to write, e.g. \"iac\"."}
                    },
                    "required": ["feedback", "channel"]
                }
            },
            "class": "save_iteration_feedback_tool.SaveIterationFeedbackCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "load_drawio",
            "function": {"description": "Loads the most recently saved cloud architecture diagram, if any. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "load_drawio_tool.LoadDrawioCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "save_iac_files",
            "function": {
                "description": "Persists the complete set of generated IaC files for this pass.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "files": {"type": "object", "description": "{\"relative/filename.ext\": \"file content\", ...} -- the WHOLE project, not a diff."}
                    },
                    "required": ["files"]
                }
            },
            "class": "iac_tool.SaveIacFilesCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "validate_iac_files",
            "function": {"description": "Validates the syntax of whatever IaC files are currently saved. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "iac_tool.ValidateIacFilesCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "zip_iac_files",
            "function": {"description": "Zips the currently-saved IaC files into a single downloadable archive. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "iac_tool.ZipIacFilesCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "download_artifact",
            "function": {
                "description": "Saves a copy of a generated artifact (here, the IaC zip) to a local filesystem path.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "target": {"type": "string", "description": "Which artifact -- pass \"iac zip\"."},
                        "destination": {"type": "string", "description": "The local filesystem path to save it to."}
                    },
                    "required": ["target", "destination"]
                }
            },
            "class": "download_artifact_tool.DownloadArtifactCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
